Kent Paul Dolan wrote:
> Yes. Sometimes to Monty Haul proportions, as with
> the addition of Alchemy to the game leading to PCs
> with 500 HPs, almost completely removing the need
> for careful turn-by-turn play.

While I agree that HP totals are getting a bit extreme (500 is by no
means the largest sum seen), the problem is merely with degree.

> > Enchantment, erodeproofing, repair, resistances,
> > cancellation and so on.
>
> And a lot of the complaining here from more
> experienced players is that the game loses a lot of
> its luster exactly because that is possible to do.

It's part of the game now, though.  Nethack has evolved since its roots
as a Rogue clone into a different game.  In some ways I think it has
evolved too much, but it is what it is.

> Well, no, issues of trading long term benefit for
> short term gains would make the player think long
> and hard about whether to take advanage of this
> YANI's implementation, at all, and that added
> richness is very much part of the intent of the
> proposal.

It would depend greatly on how the items' abilities are added, and what
those are.  It is difficult to respond to this idea in detail without
seeing it written out.  It would require careful balancing, however, to
avoid overpowering those items (can they easily get to above +7?), or
underpowering them to the extent that most players continue to ignore
them (why stick with that leather armor when GDSM will always give you
better armor class at max enchantment?).

> > with the only requirement being survival.
>
> That's always been the only requirement of NetHack.

If the only requirement of Nethack is also the only requirement to get
your stuff improved, then it seems to me that some strategy, at least,
is being removed from the game.

